> Preserve the analytic banana protocol

The source observation pools are procedural teaching data. Draw independent
standard-Gaussian pairs u,v, then set x0=u, x1=v+.5(u²−1). This triangular map
has determinant one and inverse v=x1−.5(x0²−1), so its analytic density is the
standard-Gaussian density at that inverse. Five exact points illustrate the bend
before any fitting. Both flow families receive the same 512 fitting, 128
validation and 128 test rows, with source seeds 9203,9209,9221 and source-ID
offsets 0,10000,20000. Native midpoint-MT Box-Muller draws are formed in Double;
the banana transform is cast to source Float32 observations. All 1,024 supplied
Gaussian prior rows use seed 9257. No external dataset or substitute distribution
is introduced.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pr_banana_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;namespace m=n::models;auto type=n::DType::Float64;
 auto u=n::from_vector(std::vector<double>{-2,-1,0,1,2},{5});auto base=n::stack({u,n::zeros({5},type)},1);
 auto points=m::BananaPoints(base,.5,type);auto target=n::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1.5,0,-.5,0,1.5},{5});
 if(!n::equal_bits(n::select(points,1,1),target)||n::max(n::abs(m::BananaLogProbability(points)-m::StandardNormalLogProbability(base))).item()>1e-14)throw std::runtime_error("Analytic banana");
 auto axis=n::arange(0,129,1,type)/32.-2.;auto curve=m::BananaPoints(n::stack({axis,n::zeros({129},type)},1),.5,type);
 dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(axis.to_vector<double>(),n::select(curve,1,1).to_vector<double>(),"v=0 banana curve",{37,99,235})
 .points(u.to_vector<double>(),std::vector<double>(5,0),"Base v=0",{100,116,139},5)
 .points(u.to_vector<double>(),target.to_vector<double>(),"Five native points",{220,38,38},4)
 .y_limits(-.7,2.6).x_label("u = x0").y_label("x1 = v + .5(u^2 - 1)")
 .title("Unit-volume bending gives an analytic teaching density").save_svg("build/pr_banana_toy.svg");
 std::print("Five exact banana coordinates; native analytic log densities match their inverse standard-normal coordinates.\n");
}
