> Compare complete density grids and inverse samples

Each density comparison retains all 16,384 source model cells and all 16,384
analytic-truth cells, at the original half-cell grid coordinates. Both figures
share PDF colour limits [0,.20], the same 32-colour palette and physical axis
orientation. These grids are diagnostics, not numerical normalization integrals.
The near-Gaussian learned contours are less bent than the analytic banana.
Every inverse sample and held-out row is retained in equal-scale observation
plots; the latent plots compare all supplied prior rows with transformed test
rows. Sampling uses only prior coordinates and frozen final parameters.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pr_planar_density.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;std::map<std::string,n::Tensor> data;
 for(auto& [k,x]:n::load_npz("build/planar_radial_density/planar/grid.npz"))data[k]=x;
 std::vector<double> axis;for(int i=0;i<128;++i)axis.push_back((i+.5)/16-4);
 auto panel=[&](const char* key,const char* title){auto p=n::exp(data.at(key)).to_vector<double>();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(128,std::vector<double>(128));
  for(int x=0;x<128;++x)for(int y=0;y<128;++y)z[x][y]=p[(127-y)*128+x];
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.image(axis,axis,z,dm::graphics::terrain_colors(32),0,.20)
  .margins(72,22,54,64).x_limits(-4,4).y_limits(-4,4).x_label("x0").y_label("x1").title(title);return plot;};
 dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(panel("true_log_probability","Analytic banana PDF"))
 .add(panel("model_log_probability","Final Planar PDF")).size(1100,574).fit_panels()
 .save_svg("build/pr_planar_density.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/pr_planar_density.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Analytic truth and final planar density retain all 32,768 grid cells on the same PDF scale.)
\alttext(Two square heatmaps compare the bent analytic banana density with the final model PDF.)

